fault. Inverting/disconfirming reading (named): the Wave-8 distro scan 'refuses to ship anything under .anchor/', so the leak may never be DISTRIBUTED; this narrows severity but does not defeat the finding, because C9 intends pointer-records to be COMMITTED to git for durable history, and committed history carrying an absolute path + username is itself the C10-named leak and is irreversible once shared.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Real contradiction between the stated C10 guarantee and the default-tracked content; severity bounded by the distribution-scan backstop but not eliminated for the git surface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"D2\",\n      \"kind\": \"diagnose\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"operator_wrong\": true,\n      \"reasoning\": \"OPERATOR-WRONG finding against the evident belief that C9/C10 are a clean settled 'reconciliation'. The .anchor/.gitignore denylists jobs/, *.log, *.pdf, raw/ and re-includes (!) only *.pointer.json and index.json. But the engine's actual records are discovery.json, project-summary.json and project-summary.md. Therefore: (a) the allow-list re-includes are dead rules — no emitted file is named *.pointer.json or index.json; (b) the records that ARE tracked are tracked by silent fallthrough, not by an intent rule; (c) project-summary.md (markdown) is likewise tracked with no governing rule. The classifier (gitignore) and the producer (engine output names) have drifted apart, so the policy is not wired to the artifacts it claims to govern. This is the mechanism enabling D1.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Tracking policy is decoupled from actual output naming; current outcomes are accidental, and any future rename/glob change can silently flip them.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"D3\",\n      \"kind\": \"diagnose\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lane-set drift: discovery.json's by_lane and counts include a fifth lane 'grass' (count 0), but Anchor.md lists only research/planning/build/deliverables and CLAUDE.md names only the research/plan/build 'trio'. 'grass' is undocumented — either a leftover/experimental key or a doc omission.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Schema-vs-documentation inconsistency; low consequence now (count 0) but a maintenance/clarity hazard.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"A1\",\n      \"kind\": \"anticipate\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"subject_cardinality\": 1,\n      \"future_state_condition\": \"Across many registered projects, the committed git history (and the .anchor/ pointer-records within it) accumulates absolute home-directory paths and usernames embedded in discovery.json/project-summary 'root' and metadata fields; by the time a repo is shared, mirrored, or made public, the path/PII exposure is already baked into immutable history and cannot be cleanly revoked.\",\n      \"enabling_assumption\": \"The team continues to treat the path-glob .gitignore (not content scanning) as the C10 enforcement boundary, keeps committing pointer-records as C9 prescribes, and the engine keeps writing an absolute 'root' into discovery.json.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Bounded premortem: it is several months out, many real projects are registered, and someone publishes or shares the Anchor-managed repo. The single-file leak in D1 compounds: every registration writes another absolute path + username into committed history. The author's current mental model (distribution-scan backstop) does not cover the git-history surface that C9 deliberately creates.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Forward, not-yet-present risk: leak compounds and becomes irreversible at the git-history layer the design intentionally persists.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"A2\",\n      \"kind\": \"anticipate\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"subject_cardinality\": 1,\n      \"future_state_condition\": \"After a future refactor that either renames emitted records to match the allow-list (*.pointer.json/index.json) or tightens the denylist globs, effort-history pointer-records silently stop being committed, so the C9 promise that 'effort history is durable across restarts' quietly breaks with no error and no obvious signal.\",\n      \"enabling_assumption\": \"A maintainer reads the existing allow-list as authoritative/intentional and aligns either the file names or the globs to it, assuming today's tracking outcome was designed rather than accidental fallthrough.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Because D2 shows tracking is currently by fallthrough rather than by an intent rule, any well-meaning cleanup that 'makes the gitignore consistent' can invert which records are tracked. The failure is silent (records simply absent from commits), which is exactly the kind of regression a structure-only Anchor.md index would not surface.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Latent fragility: the gap in D2 turns a routine future tidy-up into a silent loss of the C9 durability guarantee.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"S1\",\n      \"kind\": \"situate\",\n      \"severity\": \"n/a\",\n      \"rung\": \"UNVERIFIED\",\n      \"needs_verification\": true,\n      \"s0_abstraction\": \"A system partitions a stream of machine-generated records into a 'safe-to-persist' subset and a 'must-withhold' subset using rules keyed on location/name, while the sensitive attribute being guarded is a property of the records' CONTENT, and the rule-writer and the record-producer are separate components that have drifted apart.\",\n      \"candidate_field\": \"Information-flow control / taint tracking and content-based secret/PII scanning (deliberately outside Anchor's own VCS-config domain).\",\n      \"structure_map\": [\n        \"Relational correspondence 1: in taint/IFC, sensitivity is a LABEL attached at the data's point of creation and the channel enforces on the label — here the absolute path/username is a property of the record's content at write-time, so labeling-at-source (sanitize/tokenize 'root' when discovery.json is written) structurally dominates a path-glob denylist that can only guess from location.\",\n        \"Relational correspondence 2: mature secret-scanning practice assumes the denylist will be INCOMPLETE and adds a content scanner at the commit/ship boundary as the real invariant — structurally identical to D2's gap, where the path globs miss discovery.json and the only content-level check (Wave-8) runs at distribution, leaving the git-commit boundary unguarded.\"\n      ],\n      \"reasoning\": \"SITUATE fired (load-bearing C10 guarantee + a plausible better-in-class frame I cannot cite as OBSERVED + medium stakes). Answer-first: adopt label-at-source + content-scan-at-boundary from information-flow/secret-scanning practice, because the structure matches on two relational axes above. HONESTY: researchPrime was NOT commissioned (Tier-1 runtime, no agent() seam), so the base-rate/home-field viability of this frame and the map itself are UNVERIFIED and routed as a needs_verification handoff — not asserted as real, not self-CORROBORATED.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Candidate best-in-class reframe (content-labeling over path-denylist); requires researchPrime verification before it is treated as grounded.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"nitpicks\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"N1\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"'runs' is overloaded: project-summary.json reports runs:2 (apparently summary-generation/validation passes — it is 'validated, cached') while every lane in Anchor.md/discovery.json reports 0 runs. Same field name, two unrelated meanings; rename one (e.g., summary_generations vs lane_runs).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"N2\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Schema-version inconsistency across the store: project-summary.json declares schema_version 3; discovery.json carries no schema_version. Stamp a version on every emitted record for forward migration safety.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"N3\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Terminology drift: CLAUDE.md frames the workflow as a three-stage 'trio' (research/plan/build) but the store and index carry four documented lanes (adding deliverables) and discovery.json a fifth (grass). Reconcile the 'trio' wording with the actual lane cardinality.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"elevations\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E1\",\n      \"value_if_true\": \"high\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Make C10 a CONTENT invariant, not a path heuristic: write 'root' (and any abs-path/PII field) into discovery.json as a relative path or a tokenized placeholder (e.g. ${PROJECT_ROOT}) at emit-time, and add a content-level path/secret scan at BOTH the pre-commit boundary and the existing Wave-8 distro scan — so the no-leak guarantee holds wherever records are persisted, including git history (the surface C9 deliberately creates). Directly closes D1 and removes the dependence on the incomplete denylist in D2.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Proposed elevation: label-at-source + content-scan-at-commit replaces the path-glob denylist as the C10 enforcement.\",\n      \"what_would_refute_it\": \"If .anchor/ is in practice globally gitignored / never committed by any deployment and the Wave-8 distro scan is provably the ONLY surface where records ever leave the machine, then no absolute path ever reaches a shared boundary, the path-glob is sufficient, and write-time sanitization is redundant overhead.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E2\",\n      \"value_if_true\": \"medium\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Wire the .gitignore to the engine's real output: either rename emitted pointer-records to the allow-listed convention (*.pointer.json / index.json) so tracking is by explicit intent, or replace the dead re-includes with rules that name discovery.json / project-summary.* explicitly. Eliminates the fallthrough-based tracking in D2 and forecloses the silent C9-durability regression in A2.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Proposed elevation: align classifier rules with producer output names so tracking is intentional and refactor-stable.\",\n      \"what_would_refute_it\": \"If a near-term, already-planned engine change will emit records under the *.pointer.json/index.json names, the current allow-list is forward-correct and the present mismatch is transitional rather than a defect.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E3\",\n      \"value_if_true\": \"low\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Disambiguate the overloaded 'runs' field (N1) and the 'grass' lane (D3): give the summary-generation counter a distinct name and either document or remove 'grass'. Small clarity/maintainability gain that prevents downstream readers from conflating lane activity with summary passes.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Proposed minor elevation: naming/consistency cleanup of store schema.\",\n      \"what_would_refute_it\": \"If the two 'runs' fields are read only by disjoint code paths with no shared reader and 'grass' is a deliberately reserved-but-hidden lane, the overload and the extra lane cause no real confusion and the rename is churn.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"fceae531-e83d-498c-ba18-66031f19154b","total_cost_usd":0.803572,"usage":{"input_tokens":6804,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22233,"cache_read_input_tokens":204532,"output_tokens":17624,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":22233,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":13722,"cache_read_input_tokens":40615,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2479,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2479},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens"